from common import *
it('KERNEL-0302','decision','3.8','Skeleton decision (DEF-3; J-K5): built in-house (§3.1); it never writes an arena: roof ridges and hips are derived per building and cached by (entity, content revision, roof construction) - never by id alone (§8.1 (5), LC-23, N-19), so a setAttr on floors or a re-fitted footprint changes the revision and so the roof - and label spines are derived per face (§9). A stage wanting skeleton-derived topology inserts the validated arcs through insertChain like any chain. A skeleton failure can degrade a roof, a label or one stage partition, never the plat.',['§3.8 Decision'],'kernel (roof cache owned by §8.1 (5))',['@skeleton'],['§8.1 (5) roof cache','§9 label spines','@insertChain'],['skeleton never writes an arena','roof cache key','DEF-3','J-K5'])
it('KERNEL-0303','rule','3.8','Skeleton reader roof: the roofs not taking the exact path (quad wings failing the convex near-rectangle test, irregular or notched outlines - institution shapes - and stage-3 irregular wings); downgrade: a PLAIN ROOF.',['§3.8 Readers bullet 1'],'kernel (roof rendering §8 / V02 buildings)',['@skeleton'],['§8 roofs','V02 buildings'],['roof use','plain roof'])
it('KERNEL-0304','rule','3.8','Skeleton reader spine: the hole-aware skeleton graph as a medial axis - label spines (§9.3), the local feature size S1 coast simplicity clamp reads (§4.2 S1), and each room label spine and light point when the plan rung draws a storey on the building band (§2.6, §8.5, §11.1; item 28) - the plan rung only skeleton use: it takes no wall from the skeleton, so a failure degrades a room label or light and never a room (J-A28.32); downgrade: a SAMPLED MEDIAL AXIS.',['§3.8 Readers bullet 2'],'kernel',['@skeleton'],['§9.3 label spines','§4.2 S1 coast clamp','§2.6 plan rung','§8.5','§11.1','V03 interiors'],['spine use','sampled medial axis','J-A28.32'])
it('KERNEL-0305','rule','3.8','Skeleton reader regions: S10 per-frontage regions of a block (§4.7 S10 (4)); downgrade: the stage own exact construction, a REFLEX PRE-CUT PARTITION (§4.7, §5.2 F-PLOT).',['§3.8 Readers bullet 3'],'kernel',['@skeleton'],['§4.7 S10 (4)','§5.2 F-PLOT'],['regions use','reflex pre-cut partition'])
it('KERNEL-0306','mechanism','3.8','Skeleton numeric contract (GEO-8): exact rational event times are impossible (edges move at unit speed, so event times involve lengths sqrt(dx^2 + dy^2)); event times computed in doubles with + - x / sqrt only (identical on every IEEE engine; no transcendental), on coordinates relative to the polygon bounding-box corner. Input is pre-cleaned (collinear merge, minimum angle 5 deg); holes supported. Events closer than 1 mm in offset time and 1 cm in position are coalesced into one multi-edge event; ties broken by the input vertex index in the ring, canonicalised to start at its lowest-leftmost vertex.',['§3.8 Numeric contract'],'kernel',[],['@skeleton'],['skeletonContract','GEO-8','1 mm / 1 cm coalescing','minimum angle 5 deg'])
it('KERNEL-0307','constant','3.8','Skeleton work limit: 4 * (n + r) + 64 events for n vertices and r reflex vertices (a correct run needs at most about n + r); exceeding it is E_WORK_LIMIT, a count and never a timeout.',['§3.8 Numeric contract'],'kernel',[],['@E_WORK_LIMIT','@skeleton downgrades'],['skeleton work limit'])
it('KERNEL-0308','mechanism','3.8','Convex near-rectangles take the exact path (J-K21): the CONVEX NEAR-RECTANGLE TEST - a footprint wing that is convex (every turn non-negative by exact orient2d on its lattice vertices), whose four OBB corners are each matched by a footprint vertex within 30 cm and whose area >= 0.97 of its OBB - gets its ridge or apex exactly, independent of sub-cm snapping, so a 1 cm edit cannot flip a micro-ridge (after snapping only 33 % of rotated squares keep a single apex, GEO-8).',['§3.8 Convex near-rectangles'],'kernel',['@obb','@orient2d'],['§8 roofs','V02 buildings roofs'],['convex near-rectangle test','30 cm','0.97','J-K21'])
it('KERNEL-0309','rule','3.8','Exact roof constructions: a hip roof ridge ends half the OBB minor extent in from each short side, its hips running from the four matched vertices to the ridge ends; a gable roof ridge lies on the wing ridgeAxis line (§2.6: for a rect, the OBB axis parallel to the side it names; for a quad, the line joining the arc-length midpoints of the two side chains it names, sides delimited by BaseWing.corners) and ends where that line meets the footprint boundary; a pyramid apex is the OBB centre. Every roof line lies inside the footprint (endpoints do; hip ridge end or apex at least half the OBB minor extent inside the OBB, far more than the <= 30 cm a convex footprint matching all four corners can fall short; a segment between two points of a convex polygon lies inside it), so VIS-11 crossing hip lines cannot occur.',['§3.8 Convex near-rectangles'],'kernel (roof kinds owned by §2.6 / V02 buildings)',['§2.6 ridgeAxis, BaseWing.corners'],['§8 roof rendering','§11 export roof lines'],['hip','gable','pyramid','ridgeAxis','VIS-11'])
it('KERNEL-0310','receipt','3.8','Convexity is what makes the exact path true: a 20 x 10 m rectangle with a 3 x 2 m notch at x = 1-4 m on a long side (area 0.970 of its OBB, all four corners present) has a reflex vertex and so takes the skeleton (fixall_kernel_r2_attract.out D).',['§3.8 Convex near-rectangles'],'kernel',[],['§13.1 skeleton fixtures'],['notch receipt'])
it('KERNEL-0311','rule','3.8','Roofs are taken wing by wing, by BaseWing.kind (§2.6): rect wings (base outline builder rectangles and L/T compositions) are convex, so most roofs take the exact path; a quad wing (a row house cut to a curved frontage) takes it when it passes the test and the validated skeleton otherwise - no path asserts a party-wall side is straight, so a quad whose bent side (the lot cut one bend) fails the test takes the skeleton; polygon wings keep the pyramid, cone and dome constructions.',['§3.8 Convex near-rectangles'],'kernel (wing kinds §2.6)',['§2.6 BaseWing.kind'],['§8 roofs','V02 buildings'],['rect','quad','polygon wing'])
it('KERNEL-0312','mechanism','3.8','Skeleton post-condition validator: every result checked before any use: (1) planarity - no two arcs cross (exact orient2d on nodes rounded to the millimetre in bounding-box-relative integers); (2) exactly one skeleton face per input edge, monotone along that edge direction; (3) heights consistent - each node offset time equals its distance to the supporting lines of its faces within 1 cm, and times increase along every arc away from the boundary; (4) the graph is connected with exactly h independent cycles for h holes (a tree only with no holes).',['§3.8 Post-condition validator'],'kernel',['@skeleton'],['@skeleton downgrades','§13.1 skeleton fixtures'],['skeleton validator'])
it('KERNEL-0313','fallback','3.8','A validator failure, or E_WORK_LIMIT, takes the reader downgrade, reported W_DOWNGRADE: a plain roof is the footprint drawn and exported without ridge or hip lines, its heights unchanged (MFCG own behaviour when its gable pass fails); a sampled medial axis is a Voronoi of boundary samples at 1 m via d3-delaunay, pruned by clearance. Never a crash, never a roof line outside its footprint, never unvalidated output.',['§3.8 Post-condition validator'],'kernel',['@skeletonValidator','@delaunator'],['W_DOWNGRADE','§8 roofs','§9.3 labels','§11 export'],['downgrade','1 m samples'])
it('KERNEL-0314','test','3.8','Skeleton M0 (a) confirmation: runs §13.1 skeleton fixtures and the B-CITY-lite corpus of irregular footprints and district and water faces. Pass: 0 crashes; 0 validator failures on the fixtures; downgrades <= 0.5 % and work-limit hits <= 0.1 % on the corpus; <= 20 us median per footprint.',['§3.8 Confirmation at M0'],'kernel (milestone §16 M0)',['§13.1 fixtures','B-CITY-lite'],['§16 M0'],['0.5 % downgrades','0.1 % work-limit','20 us'])
it('KERNEL-0315','fallback','3.8','Skeleton pre-decided fallback: if the corpus bar fails after one repair pass, the downgrades become the default paths - convex near-rectangular wings keep exact OBB roofs, every other roof is plain, every spine reader takes the sampled medial axis and S10 the reflex pre-cut partition - and the skeleton leaves the product (kept in the testkit for comparison); nothing else changes, because the readers are the only readers of skeleton output.',['§3.8 Confirmation at M0'],'kernel',['@skeleton'],['§8 roofs','§9.3','§4.7 S10'],['skeleton leaves product'])
it('KERNEL-0316','rule','3.9','Geometry in the document (SCL-11, kernel part): the snapshot geometry (§2.9) is one block per PlatKey, never one per level; a boundary seen by several levels (a kerb, a plot line, a shore) is written once.',['§3.9 intro','§3.5 identity-keyed row'],'kernel (snapshot format owned by §2.9)',['§2.9 snapshot'],['§2.9 save/load','§11.1 compact twin'],['snapshotBlock','one block per PlatKey','SCL-11'])
it('KERNEL-0317','rule','3.9','Snapshot vertices: live vertices in slot order (slots compacted on save; nothing outside the kernel stores a slot and no output depends on slot numbers, §3.3, so renumbering is free), coordinates as delta + zig-zag varint Int32 pairs, with their current birth levels (after any promotion or demotion, §2.2 rule 3) and flags.',['§3.9 vertices bullet'],'kernel',['@slotIndependence'],['@load','§2.9'],['zig-zag varint','slot compaction'])
it('KERNEL-0318','rule','3.9','Snapshot edges: one (origin, destination, birth level) triple per edge pair, the level being the current one (the lowest among the edge rows, §2.2 rule 3); next, prev and twins not stored.',['§3.9 edges bullet'],'kernel',[],['@load'],['edge triple'])
it('KERNEL-0319','rule','3.9','Snapshot roles: (edge or vertex, role, entity, level, ordinal) rows, entities as document ids - the rows of every role §2.2 rule 7 registry marks indexed, the knot roles among them, included (an edit anchor contact names never reach a snapshot: the batch that promotes them strips them, §3.3 rule 3), so every entity-held vertex and every chain profile survives the round trip without a stored position (§2.2 rule 5).',['§3.9 roles bullet'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 7 registry'],['@load','§2.2 rule 5'],['role rows persisted','anchor never persisted'])
it('KERNEL-0320','rule','3.9','Snapshot faces: per level, one (boundary half-edge, owner, kind, stage rank) row per face; parents not stored. The stage rank is persisted because the kept-content rule (§3.3) must decide the same way after a reload as in the session that made the face.',['§3.9 faces bullet'],'kernel',['@keptContent'],['@load','@keptContent after reload'],['stage rank persisted'])
it('KERNEL-0321','budget','3.9','No per-level digest is stored (§2.9 step 3). The ordinal and stage-rank columns - 2 bytes per role row, 1 byte per face - are about 0.45 MB raw at CITY, almost all zero ordinals and a handful of repeated ranks that the document compression removes; §2.9 document-size test measures the result at M1.',['§3.9 after bullets'],'kernel (test §2.9)',['§2.9 step 3'],['§2.9 document-size test','§16 M1'],['0.45 MB'])
it('KERNEL-0322','mechanism','3.9','Chunks: the block is written as content-addressed chunks (§2.9). Per arena the LEVEL-<=1 ROUTES are one chunk: every vertex of birth level <= 1 and every L0-L1 edge as one (origin, destination, birth level) triple between its level-<=1 endpoints, with its roles of level <= 1 and the L0-L1 face rows (no finer Steiner vertex part of it). Each static-index partition (§3.3) is one chunk holding the vertices belonging to it by §3.3 rebuild rule, its edges of level >= 2 (every district boundary is an L1 edge, §2.2 rule 1, so each lies in one partition), the STEINER SPLITS its batches made on L0-L1 edges (each as the coarser edge route endpoints and the split vertex, by lattice point, with the split vertex level), the finer roles those batches added to the coarser edges sub-edges, and its face rows of level >= 2.',['§3.9 Chunks'],'kernel (chunking owned jointly with §2.9)',['@indexRebuild partition rule','§2.9 content addressing'],['@load','§2.9 rebased-size test'],['chunk','level-<=1 routes chunk','Steiner split record'])
it('KERNEL-0323','rule','3.9','Cross-chunk references: every reference to a vertex another chunk holds is its lattice point, never (chunk, index): a vertex is unique per lattice point in its arena (§3.10); a vertex on an L1 edge two partitions share (the foot of a wall- or margin-band cross edge, §4.7) is written in the chunk of the partition with the smaller key and named by its lattice point from the other.',['§3.9 Chunks'],'kernel',['@invariants one vertex per lattice point'],['@load'],['lattice-point reference'])
it('KERNEL-0324','mechanism','3.9','Chunk load order: the kernel inserts the level-<=1 routes, then each partition chunk in partition-key order - its splits first, sorted along each coarser edge by position, then its vertices and edges - resolving lattice-point references through the one-vertex-per-lattice-point index; the rebuild then yields the same arena and per-level digests (they read routes, not slots), and save -> load -> save holds chunk by chunk.',['§3.9 Chunks'],'kernel',['@chunks'],['§13.1 round-trip test','§6.2 digests'],['exactInsertion','load order','save-load-save'])
it('KERNEL-0325','rule','3.9','Chunk rewrite locality: an edit that renumbers one partition vertices rewrites that partition chunk alone (an item whose partition changes, §3.3, rewrites both chunks); the level-<=1 chunk only when it changes a level-<=1 route, promotes a vertex into one, or demotes a vertex or edge out of one; and when it changes a level-<=1 route edge, also every partition chunk holding a Steiner split on that edge, because each split names its route edge by the edge endpoints (J-DET-36) - the partitions whose faces border the edge, which an edit changing a coarse route (a carve or water edit footprint, a seam amendment two districts) mostly re-derives anyway, and which §2.9 rebased-size test at M2a counts, with §2.9 block-group fallback pre-decided.',['§3.9 Chunks'],'kernel',['@chunks'],['§2.9 rebased-size test (M2a)','§2.9 block-group fallback'],['chunk rewrite locality','J-DET-36'])
it('KERNEL-0326','mechanism','3.9','Load: the kernel rebuilds next/prev by sorting each vertex outgoing edges by exact angle (quadrant, then orient2d), re-walks faces level by level, recovers each face parent from its boundary edges and rebuilds the role reverse index and the static index partitions: O(E log d), deterministic. The per-level digests are recomputed from the rebuilt arena within the load digest check (§2.9 steps 2-3).',['§3.9 Load'],'kernel',['@chunks','@orient2d'],['§2.9 load digest check','@indexRebuild'],['load','O(E log d)'])
it('KERNEL-0327','fallback','3.9','Pre-decided fallback for §2.9 open-to-editable bar: each L2-L4 static index partition is rebuilt at the first query that reads it instead of at load - output-neutral, because every query result is sorted by its canonical key whichever structure holds an item (§3.3).',['§3.9 Load'],'kernel',['@queries'],['§2.9 open-to-editable bar','§12 budgets'],['lazy partition rebuild'])
it('KERNEL-0328','rule','3.9','Entities carry no slots (§2.2 rule 5), so the entity table needs no remapping on load. The round-trip test is §13.1; the compact twin carries the same block (§11.1).',['§3.9 Load'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 5'],['§13.1 round-trip test','§11.1 compact twin'],['no entity remap'])
add(I)
